		{
			dependencies: ["6DB0620C467D51A3"]
			description: [
				"Learn how a bee enters its hive, what flower it requires, and what angers it before scaling production. Wear protection when appropriate and provide enough enclosed space to prevent escapes."
				"Upgrade one hive at a time and verify its output. Bottlenecks are easier to diagnose in a small working apiary than in a wall of identical boxes."
				"The old field keepers believed bees judged a settlement by its patience. Whether or not that is true, patient apiaries certainly perform better."
				"Advanced Hives accept upgrades and expansion boxes, but automation magnifies mistakes. Stabilise one occupied hive, verify output, and add upgrades gradually while watching space, flowers, weather exposure, and access."
			]
			id: "4DE1D7B402C86051"
			rewards: [{
				id: "67D450B6C7F10A0D"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 10
			}]
			subtitle: "Safe handling before high output"
			tasks: [{
				id: "6B6023FBBF2A1145"
				title: "I read and understood these field notes"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "The Quiet Apiary"
			x: 1.5d
			y: 5.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"Solitary bees live in individual nests and generally serve as discoveries or breeding parents. Hive bees can live in managed hives and produce combs when their flowering requirement is met."
				"Use JEI on the bee entry to inspect spawning nests, breeding parents, flowering blocks and comb outputs. The same flower does not satisfy every species."
				"Keep at least one safe parent pair before beginning a long breeding tree. A rare bee should never exist only in the cage currently inside your backpack."
				"Productive Bees distinguishes vanilla hive bees from solitary bees that live in nests. Use the in-game bee guide and JEI to identify housing, food, breeding parents, and product processing for the species you want."
			]
			id: "2496EF07B0024685"
			rewards: [{
